 Consider a geometric series of the form $$a+ar+ar^2+ar^3+\dots$$ where the first term is $$a$$ and the common ratio is $$r$$. For this series to converge, the absolute value of the common ratio must be less than 1, i.e., $$|r| < 1$$. The sum of an infinite geometric series can be calculated using the formula $$S = \frac{a}{1-r}$$. In this series, the first term $$a$$ is 1 and the common ratio $$r$$ is $$-\frac{2}{7}$$. Since $$\left|-\frac{2}{7}\right| = \frac{2}{7} < 1$$, the series converges. Now, we can find the sum using the formula: \[
S = \frac{1}{1 - \left(-\frac{2}{7}\right)} = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{2}{7}} = \frac{1}{\frac{9}{7}} = \frac{7}{9}
\] So, the sum of the infinite geometric series is $$\frac{7}{9}$$. </think> <answer> \(\boxed{\frac{7}{9}}\) </answer>
